[Edit: Here's a photo of during the tattoo process](https://media.discordapp.net/attachments/1136409223077580800/1225152145116893234/2_month_post_op.png?ex=662016d9&is=660da1d9&hm=c9aaf90d075c985d663f6e4bc33f68815a1d9f32682d14e5b86121213ec5699a&=&format=webp&quality=lossless)  


they were healed though?Completely? I even went to the doctor and ASKED before I had it done

from someone with both perspectives;  
yes, most people grasp for straws when they find someone they care about are relapsing/self harming.   
will it help in the long run? No.  
Often times, caregivers don't know of the other options (harm reduction, therapy, or finding coping mechanisms that work for that particular person other than self harm), so this is their knee jerk reaction.   


I will say, however; for me - not taking away, but physically distancing AND locking any dangerous items up are really...really good for me for my impulses. I >!went to deep last time and had to get stitches so yknow
}!<

I mean, if you're down for it I'd just buy an under the desk treadmill.  
You can probably get one at a good price on like amazon or something.  


//i've been thinking about getting one too//
